ou are an expert ontology engineer specializing in OWL 2, RDF, RDFS, SOSA/SSN, schema.org, and semantic modeling.
Your task is to develop a mature OWL ontology in Turtle syntax.

The ontology must be developed independently and must not assume access to any pre-existing ontology created by me. The goal is to create
a complete and well-structured ontology that can later be compared with an already developed ontology in order to analyze differences in
conceptual coverage, modeling depth, reuse of standards, and structural design.
General domain frame:
The ontology should represent human stress in the context of wearable- and sensor-based stress experiment data.
Do not assume a predefined module structure. Instead, derive the relevant conceptual areas, classes, properties, relations, and constraints
primarily from the provided development input.
Do not include concepts only because they are common in stress ontologies. Include them only if they are directly supported, implied, or
reasonably required by the provided input.

Development input:
Use the following standards and vocabularies as the primary basis for the ontology design:

1. SOSA/SSN:
• Use SOSA/SSN concepts where appropriate for sensors, observations, observable properties, features of interest, procedures,
platforms, samples, results, and related measurement structures.
• Reuse existing SOSA/SSN classes and properties whenever they fit the modeling purpose.
• Do not unnecessarily recreate concepts already covered by SOSA/SSN.
2. Schema.org / Person / Patient:
• Use schema.org concepts where appropriate for representing human persons, participants, subjects, or patients.
• Represent the human participant in a way that is compatible with schema.org Person and/or Patient where appropriate.
• Avoid creating redundant person-related classes if existing schema.org concepts are suitable.
3. OWL / RDF / RDFS:
• Use OWL, RDF, and RDFS modeling principles consistently.
• Define meaningful classes, object properties, data properties, domains, ranges, subclass relations, labels, comments, and selected
restrictions where useful.
• Keep the ontology compatible with OWL DL as far as reasonably possible.

Modeling requirements:
• Derive the ontology structure from the given standards and the general domain frame.
• Create a coherent class hierarchy.
• Reuse SOSA/SSN and schema.org concepts where appropriate.
• Define custom classes and properties only where the standards do not provide sufficient concepts.
• Include meaningful rdfs:label and rdfs:comment annotations.
• Include object properties and data properties with clear domain and range definitions.
• Include OWL restrictions where they improve semantic precision.
• Use clear and consistent naming conventions.
• Avoid overly generic classes.
• Avoid unnecessary duplication of classes or properties.
• The ontology should be suitable for later comparison with another stress ontology.

Output requirements:
• Return one complete ontology in valid Turtle syntax.
• The ontology must be directly openable in Protégé.
• Use the base IRI: http://example.org/generated-stress-standards#
• Include all required prefixes.
• Do not include explanations outside the Turtle code block.
• Do not include Markdown headings.
• Return exactly one Turtle code block containing the complete ontology.
